  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Helical Piers</span>
        <h1>Helical Piers —<br /><em>Bearing Plates, Turned In</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Hearing the term on an estimate doesn't mean you need them. Here's what they are, how they carry load, and what New Mexico soil has to look like for them to be the right call.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">Helical piers are steel shafts with bearing plates, rotated into the ground until they reach soil capable of carrying structural load. They are not the same system as a push pier or a micropile, and they address one specific problem — bearing capacity — not every cause of foundation movement.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>The Mechanism: <em>Bearing Plates Turned Into the Ground</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">A helical pier is a steel shaft with one or more helical bearing plates welded to it — installed by rotating it into the ground, like a very large screw, until it reaches soil capable of carrying structural load.</p>
          <p>The system has a lead section carrying the bearing plates (typically 8 to 14 inches in diameter), extension shafts to reach target depth, a coupling system joining those sections, and a foundation bracket connecting the finished pier to your structure. Unlike a push pier, which is driven using the structure's own weight as reaction force, or a micropile, which is drilled and grouted, a helical pier is turned into the ground under torque — no dead-load requirement, no drill rig.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>Two Sources of Capacity, <em>Not Equal Contributors</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Capacity comes from two places at once, and they don't contribute equally.</p>
          <p>The helical plates create bearing surfaces at depth. Capacity increases with larger plate diameter, more plates, and placement in higher-capacity soil. Plate spacing matters: plates set too close together can overlap their stress zones and fail to develop independent bearing.</p>
          <p>Shaft friction adds to total capacity but is the less reliable contributor in most residential work — it matters more in cohesive clay than in loose granular soil. In practice, though, what actually drives a helical pier installation in New Mexico has less to do with that soil-type label than with depth and strata: TLS's owners have installed piers as shallow as 7 feet and as deep as 100 feet on different jobs, and the real difference between those two isn't "clay versus sand" — it's how deep the actual problem sits and what layers exist between the surface and that depth. A dense layer near the surface doesn't tell the whole story if a compressible layer still sits underneath it; installation torque can climb in that dense zone without the pier having actually reached past the weaker material below, which is exactly why the target is competent bearing soil the whole way through, not wherever torque happens to rise first. Helical piers see steady use in the valley regions closest to the Rio Grande — Corrales, the North Valley, the South Valley — where low-lying water tables are common.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Torque correlation</span>
            <p>Installation torque is tracked and correlated to capacity using published torque factors based on shaft diameter — a smaller square shaft and a larger pipe shaft use different, published ratios. It's standard, routine practice across the industry, not a proprietary calculation or a judgment call unique to any one installer.</p>
          </div>
          <details class="deeper">
            <summary class="deeper-toggle">
              <span>Going Deeper — For the Technically Curious</span>
              <span class="deeper-toggle-icon">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="deeper-body">
              <p>The correlation itself is a simple equation: ultimate capacity equals the torque factor (<strong>K<sub>t</sub></strong>) multiplied by final installation torque — <strong>Q = K<sub>t</sub> × T</strong>. Allowable capacity is typically set at half that ultimate value, the standard safety margin.</p>
              <p>K<sub>t</sub> isn't one number — it's published per shaft type and size, and it runs lower as the shaft gets larger:</p>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Shaft</th><th>K<sub>t</sub> (ft⁻¹)</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>1.5" / 1.75" square shaft</td><td>10</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>2.875" round shaft</td><td>9</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>3.0" round shaft</td><td>8</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>3.5" round shaft</td><td>7</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
              <p>These are the standard's default values — manufacturers verify their own product's actual K<sub>t</sub> through full-scale field load testing rather than just relying on the default, which is why an engineering evaluation report for a specific pier product can cite a different number than the table above.</p>
              <p class="deeper-cite">Source: ICC-ES AC358, Acceptance Criteria for Helical Pile Systems and Devices, Table 3.</p>
              <p>The torque number doesn't stop mattering once the pier is in the ground, either. IBC Section 1705.9 requires <strong>continuous</strong> special inspection during helical pile installation — someone recording installation equipment, pile dimensions, tip elevation, and final torque as it happens, not reconstructed afterward. New Mexico's commercial building code adopts that section without amendment.</p>
              <p>In practice, that inspection is a cost the homeowner bears directly, and on a residential retrofit it isn't always requested at the code's full standard — a real gap between what the code calls for and what a given job actually gets. Worth asking about directly if it matters to you, rather than assuming it's automatically included in a bid.</p>
              <p class="deeper-cite">Source: IBC Section 1705.9, Helical Pile Foundations; 14.7.2 NMAC (New Mexico Commercial Building Code).</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="scope">
          <h2>What a Helical Pier <em>Does and Doesn't Address</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Helical piers address load support — bearing capacity, competent soil at reachable depth. That's the whole scope.</p>
          <p>They do nothing for drainage problems, moisture infiltration, plumbing leaks, or seasonal clay expansion. Installing piers when moisture is the actual cause can stabilize elevation while the real problem keeps running underneath it. And piers respond to load-bearing distress, not cracking in general — most residential cracking is ordinary material behavior, shrinkage or thermal movement, not foundation distress. Telling the two apart is what an evaluation is for.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="installation">
          <h2>How a Pier <em>Goes In</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Installation runs on hydraulic torque motors mounted on skid steers, mini excavators, or hand-held equipment depending on access — depth is reached when the target torque is hit, practical refusal occurs, or a predetermined depth from soil data is met, whichever comes first. The target is competent soil, not maximum depth — going deeper than the bearing stratum actually requires doesn't add capacity.</p>
          <p>Helical piers are usually TLS's first choice when they're viable — soil and depth allowing, they're the most versatile option in the toolkit. But "usually" isn't "always": the depth of the actual problem and a real understanding of the soil the pier has to pass through still decide whether they're the right call on a given job, not just default preference.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Driven, not drilled</span>
            <p>A helical pier is turned into the ground — the helix advances at its own pitch, roughly 3 to 4 inches per rotation, the same way a screw advances into wood. If a crew has to auger or force the pile down rather than let it turn in naturally, that's a sign the installation isn't going the way it should. Occasional forcing can happen over a long pier, but it's a real problem if it happens right at the embedment depth where the final torque reading is taken — that reading is what the whole capacity correlation depends on, and it's only meaningful if the pier actually turned its way there.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Scope exclusions</span>
            <p>Standard installation does <strong>not</strong> include drainage correction, interior finish repair, plumbing fixes, or cosmetic crack repair. If a quote includes piers, confirm in writing what's in scope and what isn't before work starts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a> · <a href="/glossary#helical-pier" class="body-link">Glossary: Helical Pier →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

